Ilkyne is a terrestrial planet like [[Geography/Althane/index|Althane]] -- albeit slightly larger. It has much harsher weather patterns than most of Althane and is covered near-completely in dense cloud cover, making mapping the surface remotely impossible. Bursts of red lightning can be seen within the clouds, and a great shadow can sometimes be seen moving on the surface.

Beneath the cloud lies the City of Yellow, the seat of [[Divinity/Unknown Entities/The Deep|the Deep]]. It is built as concentric rings around a hill. The streets are paved in interlocking triangles, squares and hexagons, split by fissures that leak yellow light, and the buildings are faced in stone like marble and roofed in gold tiles. The air is dry and acrid and smells of rotten eggs, and a low organ note runs under everything, louder and clearer toward the hill. A long staircase climbs from the outermost ring to the top of the hill, and where it crests there is no lighthouse to be seen: a great organ grown out of fungus blocks the way, and beyond it, in a depression at the hill's top, a lake of water so deep a blue it is nearly black holds a lighthouse in its middle.

Six black pyramids stand spaced around the foot of the hill, their faces carved with spiralling yellow cracks, each with a plaque that names it in a script a reader understands without having learned it: Origin, Sky, Cycle, Geometry, History and Hierarchy. None shows a door. Each is a shaping point: touching a plaque opens a trial in which the city falls away and a room or an arena grows around the plaza, and passing the trial sends a beam of yellow light from the spire and brings another part of the city back whole. As the spires were lit the city stopped half-melting into itself, its endless stairways and crossing buildings settling into a plain and solid shape, and it grew out past its rings and filled with people. It has spread further over the planet since, and now holds tens of thousands of them.

The city's people were hunched figures wrapped in yellow burlap, shuffling through the streets, scratching at the walls and pointing at strangers, each trailing a cord that leads up toward the hill. As the spires were lit they grew varied and purposeful, taking up trades, walking in pairs and threes, and dressing in frilled and coloured clothes set with gems; some in burlap remain only in the alleys. They bow with one arm back and one arm tucked under, and keep no bank. They offer a guest nothing, saying no food they could serve would be good enough, and send anyone who wants something better up the hill to the palace for ambrosia and nectar. Their true form is a smooth gray body with three legs and three arms set radial around the torso and no face.

The crowded alien starscape that hung over the city is gone, replaced by a black, starless sky.
